Hmm — actually, using the enum as the key is better: the mapping from string→enum is fail-closed in ONE place (TryResolve), and the ceiling table is keyed on the typed enum. The policy does:

```csharp
if (!AuthorizationRoles.TryResolve(request.Principal.Role, out var role))
    return AuthorizationDecision.Deny;
...
var ceiling = AuthorizationRoles.CeilingFor(role);
if (ceiling is null || effectiveRisk > ceiling)
    return AuthorizationDecision.Deny;
```

That's clean and typed. 

Now — should the ceiling check happen in the deny chain or after effective risk computation? The effective risk computation needs to happen before the ceiling check. Currently the deny chain runs first, then effective risk, then the risk switch. Minimal restructure:

```csharp
public static AuthorizationDecision Evaluate(AuthorizationRequest request)
{
    var now = request.EvaluatedAt ?? DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
    if (!request.PolicyAvailable || request.Principal is null || !request.SessionValid || !request.DeviceValid ||
        !request.AgentAuthorized || !request.TaskScopeValid || !request.CapabilityGranted ||
        !AuthorizationRoles.IsKnown(request.Principal.Role) ||
        request.Risk == ToolRiskLevel.Critical && !request.MfaValid ||
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(request.Resource) || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(request.AllowedResource) ||
        !string.Equals(request.Resource, request.AllowedResource, StringComparison.Ordinal) ||
        request.AuthorizationExpiresAt is { } authorizationExpiry && authorizationExpiry <= now ||
        request.ConfirmationConsumed ||
        request.ConfirmationExpiresAt is { } confirmationExpiry && confirmationExpiry <= now)
        return AuthorizationDecision.Deny;

    // Effective risk = max(declared, escalated) ...
    var effectiveRisk = request.Risk;
    var (escalatedRisk, _) = ToolEscalation.Evaluate(request.Capability, request.Resource);
    if (escalatedRisk > effectiveRisk) effectiveRisk = escalatedRisk;

    // Authorization ceiling (stage 2 port of ROLE_RISK_CEILING) ...
    var ceiling = AuthorizationRoles.CeilingFor(request.Principal.Role);
    if (ceiling is null || effectiveRisk > ceiling) return AuthorizationDecision.Deny;

    return effectiveRisk switch { ... };
}
```

